import{_ as e,c as o,o as s,ah as r}from"./chunks/framework.RCw5T9E9.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Options → AI","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Options → AI"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"ai/settings.md","filePath":"ai/settings.md"}'),n={name:"ai/settings.md"};function a(d,t,i,h,l,g){return s(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[r('<h1 id="options-→-ai" tabindex="-1">Options → AI <a class="header-anchor" href="#options-→-ai" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Options → AI&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Options → AI</strong> is the section labeled <strong>AI</strong>. The gear on the <a href="/qa-tools/docs/ai/general.html">AI assistant</a> tab opens <strong>Connection</strong>. <strong>Review access</strong> on that tab opens <strong>Agent access</strong>.</p><p>The blocks follow this order: <strong>Connection</strong>, <strong>Models</strong>, <strong>Personalization</strong>, <strong>Agent access</strong>, <strong>Data masking for AI</strong>.</p><p>These choices are stored in this browser, apart from the inspector profile. <strong>Export</strong> and <strong>Import</strong> in <a href="/qa-tools/docs/options/settings_management.html">Settings management</a> include them, under <code>ai</code> in the JSON file. API keys and the <strong>Extra headers</strong> of your own providers are not written to the file and are not applied on import. Your own providers are written without them; on import a provider with the same id keeps the key, headers and confirmation stored in this browser, a new one comes without a key and asks for confirmation again, and providers that exist only in this browser stay. <strong>Reset</strong> does not clear <strong>Options → AI</strong>, including the keys and today&#39;s request counts. The screen says: <em>Keys stay in this browser and are not included in settings export.</em></p><h2 id="connection" tabindex="-1">Connection <a class="header-anchor" href="#connection" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Connection&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>OpenRouter</strong> is listed first, then <strong>Gemini</strong>. Each line says <strong>your own API key</strong>.</p><p>A saved key is shown as eight dots and its last four characters, with <strong>Remove</strong>. <strong>Remove</strong> clears that key and leaves the provider selected on the AI tab as it is.</p><p>An empty key shows a password field, <strong>OpenRouter API key</strong> or <strong>Gemini API key</strong>, and <strong>Save</strong>. <strong>Save</strong> is disabled while the field is empty or only spaces. The saved value is trimmed. Saving also selects that provider on the AI tab.</p><p>Under OpenRouter: <strong>Get a free key in</strong> <a href="https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenRouter</a>. Under Gemini: <strong>Get a free key in</strong> <a href="https://aistudio.google.com/apikey" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google AI Studio</a>.</p><p>Until a key is saved, the AI tab uses <strong>Gemini</strong>.</p><h3 id="your-own-providers" tabindex="-1">Your own providers <a class="header-anchor" href="#your-own-providers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your own providers {#your-own-providers}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Under the built-in providers: <strong>Your own providers</strong> — <em>OpenAI, DeepSeek, local models, OpenAI-compatible agents</em>. Any server that answers <code>POST {Base URL}/chat/completions</code> in the OpenAI format works: a cloud API key, a model on your machine or your own agent. Each provider is a row with its name, then the host, <strong>local</strong> for an address on this machine or the local network, the key as eight dots and its last four characters (or <strong>no key</strong>), and the tool mode. <strong>Edit the connection</strong> opens the same dialog as <strong>Add provider</strong>. <strong>Remove the connection</strong> asks once more in the row (<strong>Remove</strong> / <strong>Keep</strong>); removing drops the key, the model choices and the model marks of that provider, and the AI tab falls back to a built-in provider.</p><p><strong>Add provider</strong> opens <strong>Add your own provider</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Type</strong></td><td>A preset that fills the fields below; see the table after this one. Changing it replaces only the fields still at the previous preset&#39;s values</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Name</strong></td><td>Up to 40 characters, shown in the provider menus</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Base URL</strong></td><td>Up to and including the API version, such as <code>https://api.openai.com/v1</code>. A trailing <code>/</code> or <code>/chat/completions</code> is removed. Under the field: where requests go, or a warning for a remote <code>http://</code> address (the key and the data travel unencrypted)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>API key</strong></td><td>Required for OpenAI and DeepSeek, optional for the other types. Sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tools</strong> (Advanced)</td><td><strong>Native tool calls</strong> — the <code>tools</code> field of the API. <strong>Tools as text</strong> — for servers that reject <code>tools</code>: the tools are described in the system prompt and the model writes <code>&lt;tool_call&gt;{…}&lt;/tool_call&gt;</code>; tool results go back as user messages. <strong>No tools</strong> — the model answers from the question, attachments and the logs digest only</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Context size</strong> (Advanced)</td><td><strong>Small (8K tokens)</strong>, <strong>Medium (32K)</strong> or <strong>Large (128K+)</strong>. Smaller sizes send a shorter logs digest, cut tool results, drop earlier questions sooner and allow fewer tool rounds (6, 8 and 10)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Stream answers</strong> (Advanced)</td><td>On by default. Turn it off when the server breaks streamed responses</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extra headers</strong> (Advanced)</td><td>One per line, <code>Name: value</code>, for gateways and proxies (for example <code>api-key: …</code>). They override the <code>Authorization</code> header when they name it</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Base URL</th><th>Key</th><th>Context</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>OpenAI</strong></td><td><code>https://api.openai.com/v1</code></td><td>required</td><td>Large</td><td>Billed to your OpenAI account. Reasoning models (<code>o…</code>, <code>gpt-5…</code>) are sent without <code>temperature</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>DeepSeek</strong></td><td><code>https://api.deepseek.com/v1</code></td><td>required</td><td>Large</td><td>Billed to your DeepSeek balance. The reasoning of <code>deepseek-reasoner</code> is shown as thinking</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ollama</strong></td><td><code>http://localhost:11434/v1</code></td><td>—</td><td>Small</td><td>Start Ollama with <code>OLLAMA_ORIGINS=chrome-extension://*</code> (or <code>*</code>); otherwise it answers 403 to the extension. Raise <code>OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH</code> (for example 16384) for longer chats. Pick a model with tool support</td></tr><tr><td><strong>LM Studio</strong></td><td><code>http://localhost:1234/v1</code></td><td>—</td><td>Medium</td><td>Start the server in <strong>Developer</strong> and turn on <strong>CORS</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>OpenAI-compatible</strong></td><td>yours</td><td>optional</td><td>Medium</td><td>vLLM, llama.cpp, LiteLLM, Groq, Mistral, xAI, Together, your own agent</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Test connection</strong> lists the models (<code>GET {Base URL}/models</code>) and sends one short request with a test tool. It picks <strong>Tools</strong>: <strong>Native tool calls</strong> when the model called the tool, <strong>Tools as text</strong> when it ignored the tool or the server rejected the <code>tools</code> field. <strong>Model id for the test</strong> is optional; without it the first offered model is used. A server without a model list needs this id; it is added to the provider&#39;s models on <strong>Add</strong>/<strong>Save</strong>. A failure shows the message and <strong>Server response</strong>.</p><p>The first question to a new provider asks for confirmation again: <strong>Data sent to {name} ({host})</strong>. The text says whether the server is local, or that the data goes to that host under its terms.</p><p>Common errors of your own providers:</p><ul><li><strong>Could not reach {name} ({host})</strong> — the server is not running, the address is wrong, or (in the <a href="/qa-tools/docs/standalone.html">standalone</a> page) the server does not allow cross-origin requests.</li><li><strong>{name} refused the request from the extension</strong> — a local server rejected the extension origin (Ollama: <code>OLLAMA_ORIGINS</code>).</li><li><strong>{name}: {host} has no such endpoint</strong> — check <strong>Base URL</strong>; it usually ends with <code>/v1</code>.</li><li><strong>{name} rejected tool calls</strong> — set <strong>Tools</strong> to <strong>Tools as text</strong> or <strong>No tools</strong>.</li><li><strong>{name}: the account has no credits or quota left.</strong></li></ul><p>When a server rejects <code>temperature</code>, <code>stream_options</code> or <code>tool_choice</code> with HTTP 400, the request is repeated once without that parameter, and the parameter is left out for that server until the extension page reloads.</p><h2 id="models" tabindex="-1">Models <a class="header-anchor" href="#models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Models&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The provider menu here only chooses whose list to edit. It starts as the provider selected on the AI tab. Changing it does not change that selection.</p><p><strong>Reload the model list</strong> is disabled while the list is loading. When the list is loaded, the right side shows <strong>N of M available</strong>: <strong>M</strong> is the recommended models plus the ones you added, <strong>N</strong> is how many of them are still checked.</p><p>The provider menu lists OpenRouter, Gemini and your own providers. The field <strong>Add or search model</strong> is disabled until that provider has a key (your own providers count as connected once added). Without a key the line is <strong>Connect OpenRouter above to see its models.</strong> or <strong>Connect Gemini above to see its models.</strong> While the list loads: <strong>Loading models...</strong> A failure shows the error text; for your own providers the models already added stay listed under it.</p><p>The dropdown opens on focus. <strong>Escape</strong> or a click outside closes it.</p><ul><li>An empty field lists free models. The hint is <strong>Free models. Type to search paid ones too.</strong></li><li>Any text matches the model name or id, ignoring case, across free and paid models. The hint is <strong>All models of the provider, free and paid.</strong> Free models come first, then the name.</li><li>At most <strong>60</strong> rows are shown. The rest is <strong>N more, refine the search</strong>.</li><li>Nothing matched: <strong>No models found</strong>.</li></ul><p>A row shows the name, the id, and <strong>free</strong> or <strong>paid</strong>. <strong>recommended</strong> models are already in the list; their checkbox is disabled. Checking another model adds it. The AI tab shows added models under <strong>Added</strong>.</p><p>For your own providers the list comes from <code>GET {Base URL}/models</code>, without embedding, speech and image models, and has no <strong>free</strong>/<strong>paid</strong> badges; the hint is <strong>Models the server lists</strong>. Local providers and lists of up to 8 models offer every model as recommended; longer lists recommend up to three (OpenAI: <code>gpt-5-mini</code>, <code>gpt-5</code>, <code>gpt-4.1-mini</code>…; DeepSeek: <code>deepseek-chat</code>, <code>deepseek-reasoner</code>). The field reads <strong>Add or search model, or type a model id</strong>: a typed id the server does not list shows <strong>Add model id …</strong>, and <strong>Enter</strong> adds it too. This is how models of agents and gateways without a model list are added. <strong>Auto</strong> for your own providers picks among the listed models and switches on errors.</p><p>The list under the field is those recommended and added models.</p><ul><li>A checked row is available. Unchecking hides it from the AI tab and from <strong>Auto</strong>. The name is struck through. The status under the name can be <strong>quota used up, resets in …</strong>, <strong>not available for this key</strong>, or <strong>busy, retry in …</strong>. The title is the provider message. <strong>N today</strong> is how many requests that model had today. Badges: <strong>recommended</strong>, <strong>unstable</strong>, <strong>paid</strong>.</li><li>The last checked model cannot be unchecked.</li><li><strong>Remove from the list</strong> is on models that are not recommended. It is disabled for the last checked model. Recommended models stay in the list; unchecking is how they are hidden.</li></ul><h2 id="personalization" tabindex="-1">Personalization <a class="header-anchor" href="#personalization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Personalization&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Defaults</strong> is disabled while the block is already at the defaults: tone <strong>Default</strong>, detail <strong>Normal</strong>, routing <strong>Lite</strong>, and an empty style field.</p><p><strong>Base style and tone</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Hint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Default</strong></td><td>Balanced answers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Professional</strong></td><td>Precise, neutral wording</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Friendly</strong></td><td>Supportive, plain words</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Direct</strong></td><td>Conclusion first, no filler</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mentor</strong></td><td>Explains the concepts behind the cause</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Answer detail</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Hint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Brief</strong></td><td>Cause, key evidence and the fix in a few lines</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Normal</strong></td><td>What went wrong, the cause, the evidence and the fix</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Detailed</strong></td><td>The whole chain of events, ruled-out causes, steps to reproduce</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Instruction routing</strong></p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Hint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Full</strong></td><td>All task instructions in every request. For strong models</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lite</strong></td><td>Core rules only; the model loads the instructions of the task when needed</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Your style instructions</strong> holds up to <strong>2000</strong> characters. The placeholder is <strong>For example: answer in Russian, end with a short checklist for the bug report</strong>. The counter is <strong>N / 2000</strong>. The text is saved about <strong>400 ms</strong> after the last change. <strong>Defaults</strong> during that wait can be overwritten by the pending save, so the cleared text comes back.</p><h2 id="agent-access" tabindex="-1">Agent access <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-access" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent access&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The description is <strong>What the assistant may read and send to the provider.</strong> <strong>Defaults</strong> restores the values below and is disabled while they are already set.</p><p><strong>Attach the logs digest to questions</strong> — hint <strong>A short summary of errors and the timeline.</strong> <strong>Allowed</strong> by default. <strong>Off</strong> sends the question without that summary. The first step of the answer then says <strong>no logs digest (turned off)</strong>.</p><p><strong>Tools.</strong> Each group is <strong>Allowed</strong>, <strong>Ask each time</strong>, or <strong>Off</strong>. The default is <strong>Ask each time</strong>. <strong>Ask each time</strong> uses the confirmation on the <a href="/qa-tools/docs/ai/general.html">AI assistant</a> tab. <strong>Allowed</strong> runs the tool without asking. <strong>Off</strong> does not give that group to the assistant. Reading this documentation stays available.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Hint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Logs</strong></td><td>Event details and search over the captured logs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Page code</strong></td><td>List, read and search the scripts of the page and their source maps</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Live page</strong></td><td>Find elements on the page, why they are disabled or hidden, their components, handler code and the current store state</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Data in the logs.</strong> Each line is <strong>Allowed</strong> or <strong>Off</strong>. All three are <strong>Allowed</strong> by default. <strong>Off</strong> replaces the hidden part with <code>[hidden by AI access settings]</code>. Store, props, emit and action values that are off become a type in brackets, such as <code>[string]</code>, while names and paths stay.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Hint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Request and response headers</strong></td><td>Sensitive headers stay masked</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Request / response bodies and WebSocket messages</strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Values of store state, props, emits and actions</strong></td><td>Off: only names, paths and types</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="data-masking-for-ai" tabindex="-1">Data masking for AI <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-masking-for-ai" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Data masking for AI&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This list is applied when data is sent to the provider. It does not change the <strong>Logs</strong> tab or an HTML report. The Logs mask still runs first. See <a href="/qa-tools/docs/logs/settings.html#data-masking">Data masking</a> for how a fragment matches a key and for <code>[MASKED]</code>, <code>[MASKED JWT]</code>, <code>Bearer [MASKED]</code> and <code>[MASKED CARD]</code>.</p><p>The text under the title: <strong>Values with matching names in objects, headers, URLs, and code are sent as [MASKED].</strong></p><p><strong>Mask sensitive data</strong> is the value-pattern checkbox. It is on by default. While it is off, only the key list is applied.</p><p>The column is <strong>Key contains</strong>. The empty table says <strong>No keys — only value patterns are masked</strong>. The placeholder is <strong>Key fragment, e.g. x-client-secret</strong>. <strong>Add</strong> or <strong>Enter</strong> trims the fragment. An empty value shows <strong>Key cannot be empty</strong>. The same fragment, ignoring case, shows <strong>This key is already in the list</strong>. The row menu is <strong>Delete</strong>. Clicking a row does nothing.</p><p>The default fragments are the <a href="/qa-tools/docs/logs/settings.html#data-masking">Logs list</a> plus <code>passcode</code>, <code>auth</code>, <code>bearer</code>, <code>jwt</code>, <code>credential</code>, <code>session</code>, <code>csrf</code>, <code>xsrf</code>, <code>signature</code>, <code>accesskey</code>, <code>passphrase</code>.</p><p><code>passcode</code> is put back if the saved list does not already contain it. <strong>Delete</strong> on that row does not keep it removed.</p><p><strong>Defaults</strong> restores the key list and turns <strong>Mask sensitive data</strong> on. The button stays enabled when the list is already the default.</p>',54)])])}const m=e(n,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
